---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias9.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Research vs Development Distinction" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Research Costs – Always Expensed" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Development Costs – Conditional Capitalisation" },
        { id: "s6", label: "The Original Capitalisation Criteria" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Amortisation of Capitalised Development Costs" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Why IAS 9 Was Absorbed into IAS 38" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IAS 9 vs Modern IAS 38" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="withdrawn"
    explanation="IAS 9 (issued 1978, revised 1993) established the foundational distinction between research costs (always expensed) and development costs (capitalised if specific criteria are met). It was formally superseded by IAS 38 Intangible Assets in 1998, which absorbed and refined its core R&D capitalisation criteria within a far broader intangible assets framework."
    historical="It is retained here for historical reference and because its research/development split and six capitalisation criteria survive essentially unchanged as the core of IAS 38's treatment of internally generated intangible assets today. For current R&D accounting, always apply IAS 38."
    link="/ias/ias38"
    linkText="IAS 38 Intangible Assets"
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Distinguishing genuine future benefit from speculative investigation</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework's asset recognition criteria require a probable future economic benefit that can be measured reliably. IAS 9 was the IASC's first attempt to apply this specifically to R&D spending – recognising that pure research (investigation without yet knowing if anything commercially useful will result) does not meet these criteria, while development (applying research findings to a specific, technically/commercially viable plan) potentially does, once sufficiently advanced.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 9 MAPPED TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Probable future economic benefit</strong> – research is inherently speculative (the entity often doesn't yet know if useful output will emerge), while development follows an identified technical/commercial path with a clearer prospect of future benefit</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Reliable measurement</strong> – development costs, once specific criteria are met, can be reliably measured and attributed to a probable future product/process</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Prudence</strong> – the default position (expense unless proven otherwise) reflected an appropriately cautious approach to a highly uncertain area of spending</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 9's research/development split, and its underlying philosophy (expense unless specific reliability/probability criteria are met), survives essentially unchanged as the core of IAS 38's treatment of internally generated intangible assets today.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The two-stage classification, at a glance</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Stage</span><span>Treatment</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Research</span><span>Always expensed as incurred</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Development</span><span>Capitalised only if specific criteria are met</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--chip" style="margin-bottom:0;">This binary split, with development capitalisation as the conditional exception rather than the default, remains identical in IAS 38 today.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 9 covered</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 9 applied specifically to accounting for research and development activities and costs – a narrower scope than IAS 38's later, much broader coverage of intangible assets generally (patents, trademarks, licences, goodwill-adjacent items, and more).</div>
        <div class="note">IAS 38 later absorbed IAS 9's content as merely one part (internally generated development costs) of a far more comprehensive intangible asset framework.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know (historical)</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Research (original)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Original and planned investigation undertaken with the prospect of gaining new scientific or technical knowledge and understanding.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Development (original)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The application of research findings/knowledge to a plan or design for the production of new/substantially improved materials, devices, products, processes, systems, or services, prior to commercial production.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Research vs Development Distinction (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Where genuine "research" ends and "development" begins</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 9 recognised the distinction was often genuinely difficult to draw in practice – providing examples: research includes activities aimed at discovering new knowledge, or searching for applications of research findings; development includes designing/testing prototypes, and designing tools/jigs/moulds involving new technology.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Classifying pharmaceutical R&D spending</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A pharmaceutical company spends $2,000,000 on early-stage laboratory investigation of a novel chemical compound's properties (no defined product target yet), and $5,000,000 on designing and testing a prototype formulation for a specific drug candidate that has passed initial feasibility screening.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            The $2,000,000 early-stage investigation is <strong style="color:var(--color-text-danger);">RESEARCH</strong> – no specific product/process is yet targeted, so it is expensed immediately. The $5,000,000 prototype design/testing is <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">DEVELOPMENT</strong> – it applies existing knowledge toward a specific, identified drug candidate – potentially eligible for capitalisation if IAS 9's specific criteria (Section 6) are met.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Research Costs – Always Expensed</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">No exceptions, regardless of eventual success</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IAS 9 required all research costs to be recognised as an expense in the period incurred – there was no capitalisation option for research, regardless of how promising the investigation eventually turned out to be. This reflects the Framework's view that, at the time research costs are incurred, no sufficiently probable/reliable future economic benefit yet exists – a position carried forward identically into IAS 38 today.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Development Costs – Conditional Capitalisation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">The exception, not the rule</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Development costs were capitalised only if the entity could demonstrate all of a specific set of criteria (Section 6) were satisfied – otherwise, they too were expensed as incurred. This "expense unless proven otherwise" default mirrors research's treatment, differing only in that development offers a genuine pathway to overcome that default presumption.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The Original Capitalisation Criteria (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The direct ancestor of IAS 38's "PIRATE" criteria</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 9 required an entity to demonstrate: the product/process was clearly defined, and costs separately identifiable; the technical feasibility of the product/process had been demonstrated; management intended to produce/market the product or use the process; a market existed (or, for internal use, its usefulness to the entity could be demonstrated); adequate resources existed (or were reasonably expected) to complete the project; and costs could be measured reliably.</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Applying the capitalisation criteria</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Continuing Example 1: the $5,000,000 development spending relates to a drug candidate that has passed feasibility screening, has a clearly defined target indication, management intends to bring to market (subject to regulatory approval), a clear market exists for the treatment, and the company has secured funding sufficient to complete Phase 3 trials.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            All six criteria appear satisfied – the $5,000,000 development cost qualifies for capitalisation as an intangible asset, to be amortised once commercial production/use begins (Section 7). If any single criterion were not met (e.g. regulatory approval considered highly uncertain, undermining "intention to market"), the cost would instead be expensed.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">These six criteria are, almost word for word, the same criteria IAS 38 requires today for capitalising internally generated development costs – commonly remembered via the mnemonic "PIRATE" (Probable future economic benefits, Intention to complete, Resources adequate, Ability to use/sell, Technical feasibility, Expenditure reliably measured).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Amortisation of Capitalised Development Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Systematic allocation over the commercial life</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Once capitalised, development costs were amortised systematically over the period expected to benefit from commercial production/use of the product or process – commencing when commercial production begins, and not exceeding a period during which the associated future economic benefits are expected to be realised. This mirrors IAS 38's own amortisation requirement for finite-life intangible assets today.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Why IAS 9 Was Absorbed into IAS 38</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Folded into a comprehensive intangible assets standard</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">As the IASC developed a far more comprehensive standard covering all types of intangible assets (patents, licences, brands, customer lists, and more) – not just R&D specifically – it made sense to fold IAS 9's research/development distinction directly into that broader standard, IAS 38, rather than maintaining IAS 9 as a separate, narrower standard. IAS 9 was formally withdrawn upon IAS 38's 1998 issuance.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures under IAS 9</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The accounting policy adopted for R&D expenditure</span>
          <span>The amount of R&D expenditure recognised as an expense in the period</span>
          <span>Movement in the carrying amount of capitalised development costs during the period</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement mattered most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Drawing the line between "research" and "development" for a given activity</span>
              <span>Whether all six capitalisation criteria were genuinely satisfied</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Period expected to benefit from commercial production/use, for amortisation purposes</span>
              <span>Reliable measurement of development costs directly attributable to the specific project</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--chip" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">The research/development classification judgement was, even then, recognised as one of the most subjective areas of the standard – a limitation that persists identically under IAS 38 today.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IAS 9 vs Modern IAS 38</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IAS 9 (1978/1993) vs current IAS 38</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 9 (withdrawn)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">Current (IAS 38)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Research vs development split</span><span>Established, with six capitalisation criteria</span><span>Identical split and criteria, carried forward essentially unchanged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Scope</span><span>R&D specifically</span><span>All intangible assets – R&D is just one component of a much broader standard</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Indefinite-life intangibles</span><span>Not addressed (R&D-specific focus)</span><span>Explicit treatment: no amortisation, annual impairment test instead</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Business combination intangibles</span><span>Not addressed</span><span>Detailed interaction with IFRS 3's acquisition method</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 9's R&D-specific principle and criteria proved so durable they were carried forward essentially verbatim into IAS 38 – the main change has been the vastly expanded scope surrounding that unchanged core.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping (Historical, Under IAS 9)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Research costs incurred</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Always expensed, no exceptions</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Development costs meeting all six criteria</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Capitalised as an intangible asset</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Development costs not meeting criteria</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Expensed as incurred</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Amortisation of capitalised development costs</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Systematic, over the commercial benefit period</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 9's legacy connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 38</span><span>The direct successor, absorbing IAS 9's R&D criteria essentially unchanged within a much broader intangible assets framework</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>Governs recognition of R&D-related intangibles acquired in a business combination, a scenario IAS 9 never addressed</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 36</span><span>Governs impairment testing of capitalised development costs and any resulting intangible assets</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "PIRATE criteria", definition: "Probable future economic benefits, Intention to complete, Resources adequate, Ability to use/sell, Technical feasibility, Expenditure reliably measured – the six development capitalisation criteria, unchanged since IAS 9." },
      { term: "Commercial production", definition: "The point at which amortisation of capitalised development costs begins." },
      { term: "Research", definition: "Original and planned investigation undertaken with the prospect of gaining new scientific or technical knowledge and understanding – always expensed as incurred." },
      { term: "Development", definition: "The application of research findings to a plan or design for the production of new or substantially improved materials, devices, products, processes, systems, or services, prior to commercial production." },
      { term: "Intangible asset", definition: "An identifiable non-monetary asset without physical substance – the category into which capitalised development costs are classified under IAS 38." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
